// srgb.nv — a colour as the bytes a display is actually handed, the
// same colour as three unit numbers, and the alpha that travels beside
// it rather than inside it.
//
// WHY sRGB IS THE PACKAGE'S CENTRE
//
// Every byte triple in a PNG, a QOI file, a JPEG, a CSS declaration or
// a framebuffer is sRGB unless something said otherwise, and almost
// nothing ever says otherwise.  So the type a caller meets first is
// `Srgb8`: three 8-bit channels, gamma-ENCODED, exactly the bytes on
// the wire.  Everything else in this package is reachable from it and
// converts back to it.
//
// WHY THERE ARE TWO sRGB TYPES AND NOT ONE
//
// `Srgb8` is what a file holds.  `Srgb` is the same colour with each
// channel as a `Float` in `0.0 ..= 1.0`, which is what a blend, a
// gradient or a conversion needs, because rounding to a byte between
// every step of an interpolation visibly bands.  Both are
// gamma-encoded; neither is linear-light.  `LinearRgb` in
// `colorspace.nv` is the linear one, and the transfer function between
// them is the single most commonly skipped step in colour code —
// `colorspace.nv`'s header says what skipping it costs.
//
// WHY ALPHA IS NOT A CHANNEL HERE
//
// A colour and its opacity are different kinds of fact.  A colour is a
// point in a space and can be converted into another space; alpha is a
// coverage fraction that means the same thing in every space, so
// converting it is a category error and interpolating it in Lab is
// nonsense.  Keeping them apart makes that impossible to write by
// accident: `Srgba8` is a `Srgb8` and an alpha byte SIDE BY SIDE, with
// the colour reachable as one field, and every conversion in
// `colorspace.nv` takes a colour rather than a pixel.
//
// WHICH TYPES ARE `@value` AND WHY THE STORAGE TYPES ARE NOT
//
// A colour is a small fixed thing and `@value` is exactly the
// representation it wants — but SPEC § 14.5 admits an unboxed struct
// in a short list of positions, and a `Result` payload, an optional
// payload, a tuple element, an enum payload and a field of a boxed
// struct are all outside it.  That decides the split, and it is not a
// preference:
//
//   * `Srgb` and `Alpha` here, and the five space types in
//     `colorspace.nv`, are `@value`.  They are WORKING values: they
//     appear as parameters and returns of conversions that cannot
//     fail, and nowhere else.  Every position they occupy is a
//     supported one.
//   * `Srgb8` and `Srgba8` are ordinary boxed structs.  They are
//     STORAGE values, and storage crosses boundaries: `colortext.parse`
//     returns one through a `Result`, `colortext.named` through an
//     optional, png-nv's background chunk is an optional colour and
//     its palette lives in a struct, and image-nv puts one in an enum
//     payload.  An unboxed `Srgb8` would have made every one of those
//     signatures illegal.
//
// A LIST of a `@value` struct is a flat buffer (SPEC § 14.6), so the
// version of this package that could hold a palette in 768 contiguous
// bytes is the one that could not parse `#c0ffee`.  The API won.

// A colour as the three gamma-encoded bytes a file or a framebuffer
// holds.
//
// This is the wire format of colour: `#c0ffee`, a PLTE entry, a QOI
// pixel, the three bytes an sRGB framebuffer takes.  The channels are
// ENCODED — 128 is not half the light of 255, it is about a fifth of
// it — so averaging two of these directly is the bug that makes blends
// look muddy.  `colormix.mix` in `SpaceLinearRgb` is the correct form
// and says why.
//
// The channels are `Int` holding `0 ..= 255`.  Nothing constructs one
// out of range: `rgb8` masks and `clamp_byte` clamps, and which of the
// two a caller wants is a decision the two names make them take.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let red = Srgb8 { r: 255, g: 0, b: 0 }
//     println("${red.r}")                             // 255
// ```
pub struct Srgb8
    // The red channel, `0 ..= 255`, gamma-encoded.
    r: Int
    // The green channel, `0 ..= 255`, gamma-encoded.
    g: Int
    // The blue channel, `0 ..= 255`, gamma-encoded.
    b: Int

// A colour and its coverage, side by side.
//
// The colour is a whole `Srgb8` in one field rather than three more
// channels, which is the point: `p.rgb` is a colour you may convert,
// mix or measure contrast against, and `p.a` is a number that means
// coverage in every space and is never converted at all.  Nothing in
// this package takes an `Srgba8` where a colour will do.
//
// **The alpha is NOT premultiplied.**  `Srgba8 { rgb: white, a: 0 }`
// is a fully transparent white, not a black.  PNG and QOI both store
// unassociated alpha, so this is the storage convention the formats on
// this grid speak; `colormix.premultiply` is the conversion for a
// caller compositing.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let half = Srgba8 { rgb: Srgb8 { r: 0, g: 0, b: 255 }, a: 128 }
//     println("${half.a}")                            // 128
// ```
pub struct Srgba8
    // The colour, with no coverage in it.
    rgb: Srgb8
    // Coverage, `0 ..= 255`: 0 fully transparent, 255 fully opaque.
    a: Int

// A colour as three unit numbers, still gamma-encoded.
//
// The working form: `0.0 ..= 1.0` per channel, so a conversion, a
// blend or a gradient step keeps its precision instead of rounding to
// a byte between every stage.  Values outside `0.0 ..= 1.0` are OUT OF
// GAMUT rather than invalid — a Lab colour converted back to sRGB
// routinely lands there — and `srgb.clamp_unit` is the explicit place
// where a caller decides what to do about it.
//
// `@value`: three floats with no header, passed in registers, and it
// never has to survive a `Result` or an optional, which is what makes
// the unboxed representation legal here and not on `Srgb8`.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let c = Srgb { r: 1.0, g: 0.5, b: 0.0 }
//     println("${srgb.to_srgb8(c).r}")                // 255
// ```
pub @value
struct Srgb
    // The red channel, nominally `0.0 ..= 1.0`, gamma-encoded.
    r: Float
    // The green channel, nominally `0.0 ..= 1.0`, gamma-encoded.
    g: Float
    // The blue channel, nominally `0.0 ..= 1.0`, gamma-encoded.
    b: Float

// Coverage as a unit number, with no colour attached.
//
// A separate type rather than a bare `Float` so that a signature can
// say which of its numbers is the opacity, and so that the one place a
// caller must think about premultiplication is a type they have to
// name.  `unit` runs `0.0 ..= 1.0`; a value outside it is clamped by
// every function that consumes one, because there is no meaningful
// reading of 130% coverage.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.alpha_from_byte(255).unit}")    // 1
// ```
pub @value
struct Alpha
    // Coverage, `0.0 ..= 1.0`.
    unit: Float

// An 8-bit colour from three `Int` channels, each masked to a byte.
//
// The masking is deliberate and matches `as u8` (SPEC § 13.3): a
// caller assembling a colour out of arithmetic gets the low eight bits
// rather than an error, because every format this grid reads stores
// eight bits and has nowhere to put the overflow.  A caller who wants
// 300 to become 255 clamps first with `srgb.clamp_byte`.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.rgb8(255, 128, 0).g}")          // 128
// ```
pub fn rgb8(r: Int, g: Int, b: Int) -> Srgb8
    todo("color-nv.srgb.rgb8")

// An `Int` clamped into `0 ..= 255` rather than masked.
//
// The companion to `rgb8`'s masking, for the caller who has computed a
// channel and wants 300 to become 255 instead of 44.  Every resampling
// filter with negative lobes — `RfCatmullRom` and `RfLanczos3` in
// image-nv — produces channels outside the range, and clamping rather
// than wrapping is what stops a sharpened edge from inverting.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.clamp_byte(300)}")              // 255
// ```
pub fn clamp_byte(v: Int) -> Int
    todo("color-nv.srgb.clamp_byte")

// A `Float` clamped into `0.0 ..= 1.0`.
//
// Where a caller decides what an out-of-gamut channel becomes.  This
// package never clamps behind a caller's back on the way through a
// space, because the round trip sRGB → Lab → sRGB lands slightly
// outside the cube for most colours and silently clamping it would
// make the round trip lossy in a way nothing reports.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.clamp_unit(1.4)}")              // 1
// ```
pub fn clamp_unit(v: Float) -> Float
    todo("color-nv.srgb.clamp_unit")

// The unit-float form of an 8-bit colour.
//
// Exact: each channel becomes `n / 255.0`, so 0 is 0.0 and 255 is 1.0
// and the round trip through `to_srgb8` is the identity for every one
// of the 16,777,216 colours.  Dividing by 256 instead — which a
// surprising amount of code does — makes white 0.996 and breaks that.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.to_srgb(Srgb8 { r: 255, g: 0, b: 0 }).r}")     // 1
// ```
pub fn to_srgb(c: Srgb8) -> Srgb
    todo("color-nv.srgb.to_srgb")

// The 8-bit form of a unit-float colour, rounded and clamped.
//
// Round to nearest, not truncate: truncation loses white and biases
// every gradient dark by half a level.  Channels outside
// `0.0 ..= 1.0` are clamped here, because a byte has nowhere else to
// put them — which is why the out-of-gamut question belongs one call
// earlier, at `clamp_unit`.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.to_srgb8(Srgb { r: 0.5, g: 0.0, b: 0.0 }).r}")  // 128
// ```
pub fn to_srgb8(c: Srgb) -> Srgb8
    todo("color-nv.srgb.to_srgb8")

// A colour paired with an alpha byte, masked to `0 ..= 255`.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = srgb.with_alpha(Srgb8 { r: 0, g: 0, b: 0 }, 64)
//     println("${p.a}")                               // 64
// ```
pub fn with_alpha(c: Srgb8, a: Int) -> Srgba8
    todo("color-nv.srgb.with_alpha")

// A fully opaque pixel from a colour.
//
// The common case — a PNG without a tRNS chunk, a JPEG, a QOI file
// whose header says three channels — written once so that a codec does
// not spell `255` in five places.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.opaque(Srgb8 { r: 1, g: 2, b: 3 }).a}")        // 255
// ```
pub fn opaque(c: Srgb8) -> Srgba8
    todo("color-nv.srgb.opaque")

// An alpha byte as a unit coverage.
//
// `n / 255.0`, for the same reason `to_srgb` divides by 255: 255 has
// to mean fully opaque exactly, or compositing a single opaque layer
// darkens it.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.alpha_from_byte(0).unit}")      // 0
// ```
pub fn alpha_from_byte(a: Int) -> Alpha
    todo("color-nv.srgb.alpha_from_byte")

// A unit coverage as an alpha byte, rounded and clamped.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.alpha_to_byte(Alpha { unit: 1.0 })}")          // 255
// ```
pub fn alpha_to_byte(a: Alpha) -> Int
    todo("color-nv.srgb.alpha_to_byte")

// Whether two colours are the same three bytes.
//
// Byte equality, not perceptual equality: this is the predicate a
// codec's running array and a palette lookup want, and it is the one
// QOI's index is defined in terms of.  Two colours a person could not
// tell apart are not equal here, and `contrast.difference` is the
// question that has a perceptual answer.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let a = Srgb8 { r: 1, g: 2, b: 3 }
//     println("${srgb.same8(a, a)}")                  // true
// ```
pub fn same8(a: Srgb8, b: Srgb8) -> Bool
    todo("color-nv.srgb.same8")

// Whether two pixels are the same four bytes.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = srgb.opaque(Srgb8 { r: 1, g: 2, b: 3 })
//     println("${srgb.same_pixel(p, p)}")             // true
// ```
pub fn same_pixel(a: Srgba8, b: Srgba8) -> Bool
    todo("color-nv.srgb.same_pixel")

// The colour packed into one integer as `0xRRGGBB`.
//
// What a framebuffer word, a terminal escape and a hash want.  It is
// also the cheapest key for a palette lookup, which is why png-nv's
// encoder is expected to reach for it rather than comparing structs.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.pack_rgb(Srgb8 { r: 255, g: 0, b: 0 })}")      // 16711680
// ```
pub fn pack_rgb(c: Srgb8) -> Int
    todo("color-nv.srgb.pack_rgb")

// A colour out of one integer read as `0xRRGGBB`.
//
// The high bits above 24 are ignored rather than refused, so a caller
// holding an `0xAARRGGBB` word gets the colour out of it without
// masking first — and gets the alpha with `unpack_argb`.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.unpack_rgb(16711680).r}")       // 255
// ```
pub fn unpack_rgb(v: Int) -> Srgb8
    todo("color-nv.srgb.unpack_rgb")

// A pixel out of one integer read as `0xAARRGGBB`.
//
// The alpha is the HIGH byte here, which is the order Android, AWT and
// most framebuffer words use — and the opposite of the
// `#rrggbbaa` that CSS and `colortext.format_hex_alpha` write.  The
// two orders both exist and neither is wrong; the bug is assuming, so
// the name says which one this is.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.unpack_argb(4278190080).a}")    // 255
// ```
pub fn unpack_argb(v: Int) -> Srgba8
    todo("color-nv.srgb.unpack_argb")

// Black: `#000000`.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.black().r}")                    // 0
// ```
pub fn black() -> Srgb8
    todo("color-nv.srgb.black")

// White: `#ffffff`.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.white().r}")                    // 255
// ```
pub fn white() -> Srgb8
    todo("color-nv.srgb.white")

// Fully transparent: every channel and the coverage zero.
//
// The value a decoder fills a canvas with before compositing.  It is
// NOT what a QOI decoder's previous-pixel register starts at — that is
// opaque black — which is the kind of detail qoi-nv's `qoiop.nv`
// states rather than assumes.
//
// ```novo
// use srgb
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${srgb.transparent().a}")              // 0
// ```
pub fn transparent() -> Srgba8
    todo("color-nv.srgb.transparent")
